 The infrastructure HTTP tests use a local server with an unanswered route for timeout checks and destroy all accepted sockets in teardown. They assert `TimeoutError` and use a pre-aborted caller signal to check `AbortError`, avoiding races between response and abort timers. Ordinary probe and webhook requests allow 20 s for machine load; the timeout test also has a 20 s test budget.
 
-The Codex readiness probe-directory tests (`packages/adapter-codex/src/probe-directory.test.ts`) run each case as owned work (#466). When Vitest abandons a case after a timeout or a cancelled run, it aborts the test context's signal and goes on to the case's finish hooks while the case body may still be running. The fake CLI runner passes that signal to `runCli`, so the running fake CLI is killed and no further one starts. The finish hook waits for the body, including the adapter's own probe-directory removal, and for every fake CLI process to exit; only then does it restore `TEMP`/`TMP`/`TMPDIR` and remove the case directory, so the next case sees none of its calls. A failure the body reaches after the abort is reported with the timeout. The lifecycle regression cases drive a probe case through a simulated abandonment and are owned the same way: their own Vitest case's abort also abandons the simulated case, and their finish hook waits for the whole test body, including its fault restoration, and finishes the simulated case if an early failure left it unfinished, before the next case starts. The 5 s default timeout and the long-path, collision and creation-fault assertions are unchanged. On Windows the adapter can try to remove its probe directory before a killed fake CLI has released it as its working directory; the adapter then keeps its typed failure and leaves that disposable directory, which the case removes once the process has exited.
+The Codex readiness probe-directory tests (`packages/adapter-codex/src/probe-directory.test.ts`) run each case as owned work (#466). When Vitest abandons a case after a timeout or a cancelled run, it aborts the test context's signal and goes on to the case's finish hooks while the case body may still be running. The fake CLI runner passes that signal to `runCli`, so the running fake CLI is killed and no further one starts. The finish hook waits for the body, including the adapter's own probe-directory removal, and for every fake CLI process to exit; only then does it restore `TEMP`/`TMP`/`TMPDIR` and remove the case directory, so the next case sees none of its calls. A failure the body reaches after the abort is reported with the timeout. A run cancelled after a case has started but before its body runs, as during its `beforeEach` hooks, still enters the body with the signal already aborted; the case then starts nothing and throws the abort reason, so Vitest reports it as cancelled, as it does the cases it never started. The lifecycle regression cases drive a probe case through a simulated abandonment and are owned the same way: their own Vitest case's abort also abandons the simulated case, and their finish hook waits for the whole test body, including its fault restoration, and finishes the simulated case if an early failure left it unfinished, before the next case starts. Further regression cases enter a probe case and a lifecycle test after their simulated Vitest case was aborted, and abort a lifecycle test's own case while its fake CLI runs. The 5 s default timeout and the long-path, collision and creation-fault assertions are unchanged. On Windows the adapter can try to remove its probe directory before a killed fake CLI has released it as its working directory; the adapter then keeps its typed failure and leaves that disposable directory, which the case removes once the process has exited.
 
 Domain adversarial probes run untrusted expressions in disposable native Node processes. Their shared TypeScript source loader uses `node:module.register`, available on the verified Node 22.14 baseline, and requires no prior build. Heap caps, external watchdogs, evaluator deadlines and rejection assertions remain enforced; the loader does not patch the evaluated component.
 
